			<item>
				<title>Cueva Tanque Azul</title>
				<link>/posts/cueva-tanque-azul/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>/posts/cueva-tanque-azul/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;details&#34;&gt;Details&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;identification&#34;&gt;Identification&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Feature type:&lt;/strong&gt; Cave&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;cave-and-exploration-data&#34;&gt;Cave and exploration data&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Cave rating:&lt;/strong&gt; OpenWater&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Highest cave rating:&lt;/strong&gt; Advanced&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Vertical access:&lt;/strong&gt; No&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Exploration potential:&lt;/strong&gt; High&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Tourism potential:&lt;/strong&gt; Low&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Goes to water:&lt;/strong&gt; Yes&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Surveyed length:&lt;/strong&gt; 4000 m&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;diving-data&#34;&gt;Diving data&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Temperature:&lt;/strong&gt; 24–27 °C&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;amenities&#34;&gt;Amenities&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;hasShowers:&lt;/strong&gt; No&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;hasToilets:&lt;/strong&gt; No&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;h1 id=&#34;additional-notes&#34;&gt;Additional Notes&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Natural site in the municipality of Gibara , in the province of Holguín , Cuba , proposed to be considered a national monument. It takes its name from a sinkhole or cenote (a flooded cavern whose roof has collapsed and resembles a large swimming pool) that gives access to the largest flooded cavern discovered on the island. Its main scenic attraction is the access to the cavern itself through a sinkhole or cenote 30 meters long by 15 meters wide and 7 meters deep with transparent waters and blue reflections that give rise to its name.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
